### How did public health experts and fact-checking organizations (e.g., PolitiFact, FactCheck.org) evaluate the accuracy of the claim that 99% of people recover from COVID-19?
Answer: Public health experts and fact-checking organizations, including PolitiFact, found Trump's similar 99% claim to be inaccurate andunsupported by data. PolitiFact reported that "totally harmless" is "a strong term" and that "no definition backs up Trump's 99% figure," noting the White House provided no supporting data. The cumulative death rate from identified cases was at minimum 4.2%—far higher than 1%—and the hospitalization rate among new cases sat at around 4%, four times higher than Trump's figure. Dr. Donald Thea, a Boston University professor of global health, stated that "to cavalierly say that only 1% of infections result in problems is wildly inaccurate," citing reports of long-term complications like blood clots, chronic fatigue, respiratory compromise, and organ damage even in people with mild cases. PolitiFact concluded that "nothing says that it's harmless for 99% of the people it touches," rating the claim as false.

