## Final Judgement
## Summary of Key Findings

The evidence reveals several important points about Trump's "99% of people recover from COVID-19" claim. First, while Trump made a similar claim on July 4, 2020 ("99% of which are totally harmless"), the specific October 23, 2020 attribution is not directly confirmed in the evidence, though the substance of the claim is well-documented. Second, the actual mortality data is mixed: the global infection fatality rate (IFR) was estimated at 0.15-0.20% (meaning ~99.8% survive), which would technically support the claim, but the case fatality rate (CFR) was approximately 2.7% (meaning only ~97.3% of confirmed cases survived). Third, the claim ignores significant complications including long COVID (persistent symptoms affecting many "recovered" patients), age disparities (7.8% mortality in those over 80), and hospitalization rates (~4%). Fourth, Trump provided no data to support the claim, and fact-checkers like PolitiFact rated similar claims as false.

## Information Still Lacking

We have sufficient information to make a decision. The evidence clearly shows the claim is technically defensible under one interpretation (IFR) but misleading when other important context is considered.

## Conclusion

The claim is technically true under the most favorable interpretation—using the global infection fatality rate (IFR) of 0.15-0.20%, which would mean approximately 99.8% of infected people survive. However, this framing cherry-picks the most favorable metric while excluding critical context: the higher case fatality rate (~2.7%), the significant prevalence of long COVID with persistent symptoms in "recovered" patients, the dramatically higher mortality in elderly populations (7.8% in those over 80), and the ~4% hospitalization rate. By presenting only the survival statistic without acknowledging these complications, the claim creates a misleadingly benign impression of COVID-19's impact.

`cherry-picking`

### Verdict: CHERRY_PICKING

### Justification
The claim is rated as **cherry-picking** because, while it is technically defensible under the most favorable metric—the global infection fatality rate (IFR) of 0.15–0.20% ([pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/33026101](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/33026101/)), which implies ~99.8% survival—it selectively ignores critical context that contradicts the impression of COVID-19 as benign. The WHO-reported case fatality rate was approximately 2.7% ([WHO Weekly Epidemiological Update, Oct 12, 2020](https://www.who.int/docs/default-source/coronaviruse/situation-reports/20201012-weekly-epi-update-9.pdf)), mortality reached 7.8% in those over 80 ([BMJ](https://www.bmj.com/content/369/bmj.m1327)), and the claim omits the prevalence of long COVID and persistent post-acute complications affecting many "recovered" patients. Furthermore, Trump provided no supporting data for the figure ([statesman.com](https://www.statesman.com/story/news/politics/elections/2020/07/07/fact-check-are-99-of-coronavirus-cases-lsquototally-harmlessrsquo-as-trump-said/42604755/)), and fact-checkers such as PolitiFact rated similar "99% harmless" claims as false, noting that "nothing says that it's harmless for 99% of the people it touches." By presenting only the most favorable survival statistic while excluding higher fatality rates, age-related risks, and long-term complications, the claim creates a misleadingly benign picture of the disease.